WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.440
From beloved arthouse classics&nbsp;
to forgettable horror revivals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.060
these franchises couldn't wait a full twelve&nbsp;
months before releasing their next installments.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.860
There are sticklers out there who will tell&nbsp;
you that The Man with No Name — the strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.600
quiet anti-hero played by Clint Eastwood&nbsp;
in Sergio Leone's trilogy of landmark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.960
Westerns — does have a name. In fact, he&nbsp;
has three names. In 1964's "A Fistful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.520
Dollars" — wherein our man pits the rival&nbsp;
factions of a small frontier town against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.880
one another — his name is Joe. In 1965's "For a&nbsp;
Few Dollars More," however, his name is Monco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.580
and he's a bounty hunter who joins forces with a&nbsp;
rival to take down a Mexican bank robber. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.920
in the 1966 semi-prequel "The Good, the&nbsp;
Bad, and the Ugly," he is called Blondie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.500
as he races against a ruthless mercenary and a&nbsp;
wily bandit to find a stash of Confederate gold.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.180
You might have noticed that Eastwood isn't&nbsp;
really the same character in each of these films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.020
nor were they released in the same&nbsp;
year. After the spaghetti Westerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.340
made waves in Italy and across Europe,&nbsp;
United Artists released them all in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.080
1967 — the first two in January and&nbsp;
May, and "The Good, the Bad, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.420
Ugly" in December. The artwork for the American&nbsp;
releases emphasized that the films were all of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.580
piece, and introduced the moniker of "The Man&nbsp;
with No Name" in an attempt to bind the films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.320
more closely together. The marketing campaign&nbsp;
worked like gangbusters — and all these years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.300
later, the three films are still packaged&nbsp;
together as "The Man with No Name" trilogy.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:37.440
The circumstances that led to both Roger Moore&nbsp;
and Sean Connery playing James Bond in 1983&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.500
are complicated and go back several decades&nbsp;
earlier. In the '50s, Bond author Ian Fleming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.020
collaborated with writer Kevin McClory on an&nbsp;
unused 007 film script that eventually became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:55.740
the novel "Thunderball." Later, in 1961, McClory&nbsp;
sued Fleming and eventually was awarded a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:02.760
credit on the novel's 1965 film adaptation, as&nbsp;
well as ownership of certain story elements — most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.640
notably, the terrorist organization SPECTRE&nbsp;
and its cat-loving leader Ernst Blofeld.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.740
McClory spent more than a decade in various&nbsp;
legal battles with United Artists in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:19.560
make his own film, and in 1983 he finally got&nbsp;
his chance. "Never Say Never Again" — produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.780
for Warner Bros. — is essentially a remake of&nbsp;
"Thunderball," with SPECTRE once again holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:30.180
the world hostage with a pair of nuclear warheads.&nbsp;
Its ace in the hole, of course, is Sean Connery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.880
returning to the role he made famous for the&nbsp;
first time since 1971's "Diamonds Are Forever."

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:39.594
"I hope we're gonna have some&nbsp;
gratuitous sex and violence."&nbsp;

00:02:39.594 --> 00:02:41.208
"I certainly hope so too."

00:02:41.208 --> 00:02:46.920
The film also boasts solid action scenes from&nbsp;
director Irvin Kershner and a great performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.980
from Barbara Carrera as the scenery-chewing&nbsp;
femme fatale Fatima Blush. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.780
the official Bond franchise kept chugging&nbsp;
along in the same year with the very silly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:04.200
"Octopussy," which has 007 — played by Roger Moore&nbsp;
in his sixth appearance — foiling a rogue Soviet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.720
general and an exiled Afghan prince with the help&nbsp;
of some beautiful circus-trained jewel thieves.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.380
In 1984, the schlockmeisters at Troma&nbsp;
Entertainment scored a genuine hit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:22.740
the superhero parody "The Toxic Avenger," in which&nbsp;
wimpy janitor Melvin Junko is dumped into a vat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.320
toxic waste and emerges as a monster who dedicates&nbsp;
himself to fighting crime. Five years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:34.440
"the first superhero from New Jersey" returned&nbsp;
in "The Toxic Avenger Part II." In the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.060
Toxie travels to Japan to find his&nbsp;
long-lost father, leaving Tromaville&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.940
helpless to the machinations of the supervillain&nbsp;
corporation Apocalypse Incorporated. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.034
though, he comes back to save the day. After all…

00:03:48.034 --> 00:03:53.314
"If Tromaville was destroyed, there would&nbsp;
never be a 'Toxic Avenger Part III!'"

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:01.500
The original cut of "Part II" reportedly ran&nbsp;
close to four hours, so Troma mastermind Lloyd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.780
Kaufman did the only sensible thing and made&nbsp;
two movies out of it. "Part III" — subtitled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.740
"The Last Temptation of Toxie" — picks&nbsp;
up at the second half of the story.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:17.040
When Toxie returns from Japan and runs Apocalypse,&nbsp;
Inc. out of town, he finds that he did his job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.440
too well, as there are no more bad guys left&nbsp;
in Tromaville to defeat. In need of a job to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.600
pay for a medical operation that could restore&nbsp;
his blind girlfriend's sight, Toxie does the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.580
unthinkable and takes a job with Apocalypse,&nbsp;
Inc. But will success and the trappings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:38.700
yuppie life spoil our lumpy protector? And is&nbsp;
the chairman of the evil corporation even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:44.400
evil than previously advertised? The "Toxic&nbsp;
Avenger" saga has to be seen to be believed.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.740
Polish filmmaker Krzysztof Kieślowski's&nbsp;
"Three Colours" trilogy consists of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.360
three films that have mostly unrelated&nbsp;
characters and plots, but are united in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.760
theme and intended as a singular experience.&nbsp;
After all, when U.S. critics were preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.680
their best-of lists for 1994, the films&nbsp;
took up one ranking spot instead of three.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.180
The title of each installment refers to&nbsp;
the three colors of the French flag and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.140
the ideals they represent: blue&nbsp;
for liberty, white for equality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:19.800
and red for fraternity. "Blue" arrived in&nbsp;
late 1993, starring Juliette Binoche as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.800
wife and mother who is cruelly "liberated" by the&nbsp;
death of her husband and young child. Struck numb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.900
by grief, she strikes up several unexpected&nbsp;
connections as she struggles to move forward.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:37.740
"White" and "Red" were both released in 1994. The&nbsp;
former concerns an unhappy man who, after getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:43.200
a divorce from his wife, smuggles himself back&nbsp;
home to Poland inside a large suitcase. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.520
"Red" follows a model living in Geneva&nbsp;
who connects with a retired judge after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.080
she accidentally hits his dog with her&nbsp;
car. Each film has the lean efficiency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.549
and finely tuned irony of a good short story,&nbsp;
and none of them quite turn out as expected.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:04.980
No other single film did more to define sci-fi&nbsp;
in the 21st century than the Wachowskis' 1999 hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.800
"The Matrix." The dystopian-martial arts-superhero&nbsp;
mashup coined a whole new cinematic language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.600
and its impact can still be seen in its&nbsp;
aesthetics, special effects technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.300
and — perhaps unfortunately — in real life&nbsp;
today. A sequel was all but guaranteed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.520
but the task of living up to fans' massive&nbsp;
expectations was daunting. Four years after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.340
Neo flew through the air at the end of the first&nbsp;
film, the Wachowskis returned with not one sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:38.640
but two: "The Matrix Reloaded" in May 2003&nbsp;
and "The Matrix Revolutions" six months later.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.800
The films promised everything a sequel should&nbsp;
have — like bigger and better action set pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.480
and more of the characters we loved from the&nbsp;
first film — along with a few answers to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.760
many questions posed by its increasingly&nbsp;
convoluted mythology. But while the sequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.560
delivered the goods when it came to action — the&nbsp;
highway chase in "Reloaded" remains an all-timer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.840
decades later — it fared less well when trying&nbsp;
to untangle its own dorm-room philosophical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.760
inquiries. "Revolutions" was largely seen&nbsp;
as a disappointing capper to the trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.020
and when Lana Wachowski returned 18&nbsp;
years later to helm the reboot "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:18.240
Matrix Resurrections," she crafted a proper&nbsp;
ending as much as she did a new beginning.

00:07:18.414 --> 00:07:23.814
"That's the thing about stories…&nbsp;
they never really end, do they?"

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:30.120
The 2000 Canadian horror flick "Ginger Snaps"&nbsp;
took on the werewolf genre through the lens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:36.060
female adolescence and puberty, as spooky teen&nbsp;
sisters Ginger and Brigitte work to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:43.500
Ginger's violent monthly urges after she is bitten&nbsp;
by a werewolf. The 2004 sequel, "Ginger Snaps 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.440
Unleashed" takes a page from "A Nightmare on Elm&nbsp;
Street Part 3" as Brigitte, now infected herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.960
tries to resist her transformations while&nbsp;
staying in a rehab facility for teens. Ginger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.640
returns from beyond the grave, appearing&nbsp;
to Brigitte in visions with ghostly advice.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:04.860
Filmed simultaneously with "Unleashed," the&nbsp;
prequel "Ginger Snaps Back: The Beginning"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:11.880
offers a frontier variation on the series. Stars&nbsp;
Katharine Isabelle and Emily Perkins return not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.980
as the Ginger and Brigitte we know and love from&nbsp;
the first two films, but as their 19th-century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:22.380
ancestors — also named Ginger and Brigitte — who&nbsp;
find themselves orphaned and lost in the Canadian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.840
wilderness. Where werewolves are a symbol for&nbsp;
menstruation in the first film and addiction in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.960
the second, "Ginger Snaps Back" uses the genre's&nbsp;
sturdy framework to examine Canada's history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:39.480
colonialism and indigenous extermination. Even&nbsp;
though the sequels weren't particularly beloved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:44.340
they weren't particularly hated either — and&nbsp;
they both make for entertaining viewing nowadays.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.220
The tongue-in-decaying-cheek "Return of the&nbsp;
Living Dead" series ran for three bloody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:56.820
campy entries from 1985 to 1993. But just like&nbsp;
a corpse exposed to the military-grade poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:02.280
gas Trioxin, the series rose from the&nbsp;
grave more than a decade later. In 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.840
two brand new "Return of the Living Dead"&nbsp;
sequels were unleashed upon the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:11.700
putting a new generation of teens in the&nbsp;
path of shambling corpses hungry for brains.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.340
"Necropolis" — the first of the new sequels —&nbsp;
follows a group of high school students whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:23.160
friend is kidnapped by a nefarious corporation&nbsp;
doing research on reanimated bodies. Actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.140
frequent documentary narrator Peter Coyote guest&nbsp;
stars as the doctor in charge of the experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.920
and he appears once again in the&nbsp;
follow-up "Rave to the Grave."

00:09:32.234 --> 00:09:35.294
"What is all this for?"
"Isn't it obvious?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.680
World domination! What's everything&nbsp;
ultimately for? Heh heh."

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.540
Taking place a year after the zombie outbreak&nbsp;
that ended "Necropolis," "Rave to the Grave"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.340
follows the surviving high schoolers —&nbsp;
now in college — as a stray canister of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:55.380
Trioxin gets synthesized into a party drug&nbsp;
that starts turning ravers into zombies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:00.480
Beyond using Trioxin as the source of&nbsp;
the zombie scourge, the films — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.380
wound up going straight to the Syfy Channel&nbsp;
— bear little resemblance to the first three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.760
lacking their punk rock sensibility&nbsp;
and love for gooey, goopy monsters.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:16.380
Kiyoshi Kurosawa's 2001 horror-tragedy "Pulse"&nbsp;
was part of a wave of internationally acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:21.480
Japanese spine-tinglers released at the turn&nbsp;
of the millennium. Naturally, its inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:27.600
American remake — directed by Jim Sonzero and&nbsp;
co-written by Wes Craven — arrived in theaters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:33.420
in 2006. It stars Kristen Bell as a university&nbsp;
student who discovers that her boyfriend's death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:38.820
was caused by a supernatural computer virus that&nbsp;
allows the dead to contact the living through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:42.900
internet. Like the Japanese original, the&nbsp;
remake has the courage of its convictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:48.180
ending on a note of defeat as the virus takes&nbsp;
hold and the spirits of the dead invade the earth.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:54.900
The two "Pulse" sequels, both released in 2008,&nbsp;
take place in a world overrun with ghosts where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.960
the living now exist as refugees in their own&nbsp;
homes. "Pulse 2: Afterlife," which features a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:06.390
man trying to protect his daughter from the ghost&nbsp;
of his vengeful ex-wife, is nearly unwatchable —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:11.040
every scene appears to have been filmed in&nbsp;
front of a green screen. "Pulse 3" doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.740
fare much better visually or narratively: this&nbsp;
post-apocalyptic take on the material features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:21.840
a college student navigating the new world and a&nbsp;
young woman who somehow discovers the world's last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.700
working laptop. You should probably click away&nbsp;
from these sequels if you ever run into them.

00:11:26.901 --> 00:11:34.808
"I want to turn it off, but… I can't."

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.860
Swedish crime writer Stieg Larsson's posthumously&nbsp;
released "Millennium" trilogy became a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:46.320
sensation when the first novel, "The Girl with the&nbsp;
Dragon Tattoo," was published in English in 2008.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:52.320
A feature film adaptation from Swedish production&nbsp;
company Yellow Bird followed in early 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:57.540
along with adaptations of the other two novels in&nbsp;
the series, "The Girl Who Played with Fire" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:02.700
"The Girl Who Kicked the Hornets' Nest," later in&nbsp;
the year. The complete trilogy was given a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:08.520
theatrical run in the United States in 2010 by the&nbsp;
distribution arm of Chicago's Music Box Theater.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:14.640
Starring Noomi Rapace as hacker Lisbeth&nbsp;
Salander and Michael Nyqvist as intrepid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.980
journalist Mikael Blomkvist, the films are a&nbsp;
pitch-black journey into the sins of Sweden's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:25.980
past. For international audiences, it's also&nbsp;
possibly their first taste of the uniquely dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:32.640
sexy, and disturbing genre of Scandinavian crime&nbsp;
fiction. Director Niels Arden Oplev doesn’t shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:38.400
away from the sexual violence that is endemic&nbsp;
to Larsson's work — the original Swedish title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:43.860
of "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo" translates&nbsp;
to "Men Who Hate Women," after all. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:49.140
sequels may not have the same visceral thrill as&nbsp;
the first film, all three were released together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:54.360
to paint a bleak portrait of a culture that has&nbsp;
yet to reckon with the rottenness at its core.

